First Confused Ascension Post :P

(Blatant spoiler warning)

I started out this character with the idea of playing wishless,
genoless, polyfooless, and confused (where confused means you start
out being permanently confused and anything that cures confusion
breaks the conduct).  I played using Andreas Dorn's conduct patch, my
conduct patch-patch (for the confusion), and the heck1d patch (for a
better Hell).  I kept all of those conducts but the wishless, mostly
because I just didn't have the patience to farm out the items I
desperately needed at the end.

In retrospect, "confused" conduct isn't so bad.  It is kind of like
taking parts of illiterate, zen, atheist, petless, and hornless
conducts and mixing them all together.  The biggest issue is the 20%
chance that any direction you pick will pick a random direction.  I
would like to thank the Dev Team right now that they did not allow "."
as a random direction to be picked.  I would hate to zap a wand of
death at a monster only to "confusedly" zap myself with it. As it
stood, I was a giant trap magnet:  I would try to move away from traps
and Uanda would run right into them even from as far as three squares
away.  I would run away into the hallway from the giant horde of orcs
and Uanda, confused barbarian that she was, would move right into the
thick of them.  Lovely.  My patience was tried with this character who
just didn't want to go the way I wanted her to go.  The boots of
shopping, err...jumping, help a lot.  Are they magical such that you
can always pick the right direction even if stunned or confused? Or,
do they just help you jump so that really sometimes you should jump
the wrong way? (which doesn't happen now.)

Overall, this game went quite smoothly.  I found a lot of decent armor
early on.  Despite the fact that you might think Sokoban is hard when
confused, this game put me up to eight for eight in confused Sokoban
solving and gave me an amulet of reflection.  Ludios netted me a wand
of death and a +2 cloak of protection.  I got Magicbane and Mjollnir
from Crom and so I was feeling really set for the rest of the game.  I
was doing really well until I got to a level below Orcus and got
attacked by a master mind flayer.  6 int is really scary to have and
so I confused teleported out (is there any other kind?) and made about
30 potions of full healing while I was there.  This was when the
scariest moment of the game happened.

I got back to the psuedo-town with the master mind flayer, by dropping
down with a cockatrice corpse from about three levels up through the
same trap door.  I took out the flayer and the corpse promptly
disintegrated a few turns later.  I went up a level to meet Orcus, who
I drug back down with me and stood on the staircase.  Well, let's just
say I did my best to stand on the staircase, but Uanda liked to
"uanda" on and off of it with great frequency.  Orcus summoned two
master mind flayers and so I started off walking towards the down
staircase away from them.  Yeenoghu got summoned.  I thought: huh,
that's no good.  I kept running away and about three seconds later,
there was a weird kelp looking thing on my screen.  (I play with
tiles).

No no, what is that?  Wait, not kelp.  Oh fudge.  Oh yeah, the
deadliest kelp of them all, Demogorgon.  Suddenly, I was feeling a lot
happier that I had just made a bag full of full healing potions.  The
two leaves in my bag wouldn't have lasted long.  A little bit of
teleporting, digging, and poetry dropping left me on the level below
with three evil demons, but no giant horde of monsters.  I was running
out of illness curing and I didn't trust myself to stand on the poetry
when confused long enough to kill three demons that kept teleporting
back to the stairs to heal.  They started to create a small horde of
monsters around me, not all of which liked my poetry.  Feeling chancy,
I broke a wand of teleport. Only Orcus was near me, and so I took a
step away, read a scroll of teleport and was back to level 18.  Phew. 
On my return, patience on the level above gave me a cockatrice corpse,
which promptly solved the demon problem.  Amazingly, I found out not
too long after that that I had been slowed by a shade from Orcus Town
and had been fighting Demogorgon without any speed at all.  Eek.

"Tough" Vlad was a pushover and barely scratched me.  I might assume
that some of it was "blind luck", but I really thought he was upped in
damage too.  At least he didn't turn me to stone.  :P

The RNG was both nice (+2 cloaks of protection are lovely) and
extremely mean.  The only bag of holding was found on the vibrating
square level.  There were no helms of opposite alignment and only two
scrolls of gold detection (one of which was read to ID it).  I only
had one wand of death that had been zapped four times already and so I
had no idea how many charges were left.  I really wanted to stay
wishless, but I didn't have the patience to kill half a million
monsters to get any of those items.  So, I wished for the helm, a
magic marker, and 3 wands of death.

As much as I wanted to stay wishless, a 200 HP character wielding +0
weapons with no wands of death is not a pretty sight against the
Riders nor while fumbling around on the planes trying to find the
portal using just the Amulet.  It was enough of a pain as it was. 
With those items and a decent AC, I was ready to go.  Luckily I had
managed to get about -23 AC if I wasn't dual wielding which was more
than I had hoped, even wearing dragon /scales/. The first high altar
wasn't the right one, but the helm of opposite alignment helped.  I
thought it appropriate that Uanda, the poor confused barbarian that
she was, would go offer the Amulet to somebody else other than her own
god.  I made about 150k moves, which isn't terribly high considering
that I tried on nearly every piece of armor in the dungeon to eek out
that extra point of AC.  I think Uanda16 makes six for me.  Oddly
enough, this is my second ascension on Christmas Day.

Other thoughts: I just want to say that hands down bar none the Heck
patch is by far the most enjoyable patch I have ever played.  If ever
there was such a large (in terms of changing a lot) patch that
deserved to be part of the actual distribution, this is it.  I don't
think I will ever go back to playing normal Gehennom again.  Lava
levels, town-ish levels, cave-like levels, slightly flooded levels,
souped up antechambers for demons, another sanctum--it's absolutely
wonderful.

Also, to be honest I only won because I fixed some bugs in the middle
of my game.  It's quite a relief (well, relief that you realized
before you got there) when, before the invocation but after offing the
Wizard, you realize that not only can you not read the Book when
confused (whoops) but also that the heck1d patch that you applied
doesn't attach the sanctum correctly to the dungeon (i.e. no Amulet
created).  I fixed both of those problems mid-game.  There was also a
weird Heck bug where a trapped door in a psuedo-town level allowed me
to walk through the door itself (as if it wasn't there).  I found it
was trapped when I tried to lock it, curious if that would stop me
from walking through it.  If Kelly Bailey doesn't respond to this,
I'll email a bug report.  I'm just curious if anybody else has had
these problems, or if it was just something I did wrong on my end. 
